    "title": "New Somali refugees in Dadaab face hardship without aid, livelihoods",
    "original_title": "Families fleeing Somalia crises find bleak prospects in Dadaab refugee camps",
    "slug": "new-somali-refugees-in-dadaab-face-hardship-without-aid-livelihoods",
    "summary": "Families escaping conflict, evictions, and drought in Somalia arrive in Kenya's Dadaab camps, only to find no assistance or means to earn a living.",
    "content": "Families fleeing various crises in southern Somalia have been arriving at the Dadaab refugee camps in Kenya, where they encounter severe hardship with no access to livelihood opportunities or humanitarian aid. The new arrivals include those escaping conflict in Baidoa, government house demolitions in Mogadishu, and drought conditions in their home regions. According to Radio Ergo, these families report receiving no reception support, shelter, or other forms of assistance upon arrival.\n\nSome of the new arrivals are staying with relatives who are already struggling to meet their own basic needs, while others have settled independently on vacant land in Dadaab's Ifo-2 camp. The lack of formal support leaves them without shelter, food, or income-generating options, compounding their vulnerability.\n\nThe Dadaab camps, established in the early 1990s, have long hosted Somali refugees fleeing conflict and drought. Over the years, the camps have faced chronic overcrowding and funding shortfalls, and recent arrivals often find themselves without the minimal assistance available to registered refugees. The situation reflects the broader challenges of displacement in the region, where prolonged crises and limited humanitarian access exacerbate suffering.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these developments are deeply concerning. Many diaspora members have relatives in Dadaab or in the regions from which these families are fleeing. The lack of support for new arrivals not only affects the refugees themselves but also places additional strain on existing camp residents, some of whom are diaspora-supported. This situation underscores the need for increased humanitarian funding and durable solutions to displacement.\n\nThe absence of reception support and aid for new arrivals highlights gaps in the humanitarian response to ongoing displacement in Somalia and the region. Without intervention, these families face heightened risks of malnutrition, disease, and further displacement. The diaspora community, often a lifeline for relatives in the camps, may find itself stretched thin as it tries to assist both new arrivals and existing residents.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/10/families-fleeing-somalia-crises-find-bleak-prospects-in-dadaab-refugee-camps/",
