    "summary": "A drone attack on a market area in Al-Dibab, West Kordofan, Sudan, has left at least 80 people dead, with concerns that Somali nationals may be among the victims.",
    "content": "At least 80 people have been killed in a drone strike near a market in the town of Al-Dibab, located in Sudan's West Kordofan state, according to medical sources and a witness who spoke to AFP on Monday. The attack occurred on Sunday evening and targeted a vehicle gathering point where people assemble to return to their villages after a day at the market.\n\nThe strike is part of the ongoing conflict in Sudan, which has escalated in recent months between rival military factions. West Kordofan has seen increased violence, though specific details about the perpetrators or the intended target of this attack have not been independently verified. A witness, identified as Ahmed Abakar Wadi, said he participated in the burial of 50 bodies and that many more casualties were reported.\n\nThe United Nations and humanitarian organizations have repeatedly warned about the rising civilian toll in Sudan's conflict, which has displaced millions and created a severe humanitarian crisis. The use of drones in populated areas has raised particular alarm due to the risk of mass casualties.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this incident is especially concerning because Sudan hosts a significant Somali community, including students, traders, and refugees. Many Somalis in the diaspora have relatives in Sudan, and news of the strike has sparked fears that Somali nationals may be among the dead or injured. The Somali government has not yet issued a statement on the incident, but community leaders are urging authorities to verify whether any Somalis were affected.\n\nThis event underscores the broader instability in the region, which continues to affect Somali communities both within Somalia and across the diaspora. As details emerge, families in the diaspora are anxiously seeking information about their loved ones. The international community has called for restraint and protection of civilians, but the situation remains volatile.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/19/suudaan-duqayn-dishay-80-qof-iyadoo-laga-cabsi-qabo-in-dad-soomaali-ah-ay-ku-jiraan-khasaaraha/",
