    "original_title": "Somalia angry as Ethiopia refuses to intervene in South West State conflict",
    "slug": "somalia-criticizes-ethiopia-for-not-intervening-in-south-west-state-conflict",
    "summary": "Somalia's federal government is angry with Ethiopia for declining to intervene in South West State, where former leader Abdiasis Laftagaren's forces captured five strategic towns in a week.",
    "content": "The Somali federal government has expressed strong displeasure with Ethiopia for not intervening in the conflict in South West State, where forces loyal to former state leader Abdiasis Laftagaren seized five strategic towns within a week, according to officials. Mogadishu had requested that commanders of Ethiopian troops in Somalia join the fighting on the side of federal forces and defend key towns where Ethiopian troops have been stationed for over a decade. Ethiopia has not acceded to the request, prompting frustration in the capital.\n\nThe tensions stem from the federal government's decision in March to remove Laftagaren from power by force. Since then, Laftagaren's forces have regrouped and launched a counter-offensive, overrunning several towns in South West State. The federal government had specifically asked Ethiopian troops to defend the major towns they garrison, but Ethiopian commanders have reportedly declined to engage in the internal fight.\n\nEthiopia is a major contributor to the African Union peacekeeping mission in Somalia and maintains its own troops in the country under a bilateral arrangement. Its forces have been present in South West State for years, primarily to combat al-Shabab militants. The federal government's request for Ethiopian intervention underscores the reliance on foreign troops to maintain control in regions where federal authority is contested.\n\nThe refusal by Ethiopia comes amid heightened tensions between Mogadishu and Addis Ababa over a recent port deal between Ethiopia and the breakaway region of Somaliland, which Somalia considers a violation of its sovereignty. That dispute has strained relations and may have influenced Ethiopia's decision not to take sides in the South West State conflict.\n\nThe situation in South West State is of particular concern to the Somali diaspora, many of whom have family members in the region and closely follow political developments. The loss of strategic towns to Laftagaren's forces could destabilize the state and affect upcoming elections. Diaspora communities in the United States, Europe, and the Middle East often send remittances and invest in the region, and prolonged conflict could disrupt these flows. The federal government's anger with Ethiopia also risks further regional fragmentation, a prospect that worries many Somalis abroad who hope for a unified and stable Somalia.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-angry-ethiopia-refuses-intervene-south-west-state/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
    "source_name": "Somalia Guardian",
    "original_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-angry-ethiopia-refuses-intervene-south-west-state/",
    "country": "Somalia",
