    "slug": "sodma-fao-norway-discuss-el-ni-o-preparedness-in-somalia",
    "summary": "Somalia's disaster management agency met with FAO and Norwegian officials to strengthen early warning systems and emergency response for El Niño-related risks.",
    "content": "The chairman of Somalia's National Disaster Management Agency (SoDMA), Mahamud Moallim Abdulle, held a meeting in Mogadishu with Rein Paulsen, Director of FAO's Emergency and Resilience Division, Etienne Peterschmitt, FAO Representative in Somalia, and representatives from the Norwegian government. The discussions centered on enhancing cooperation on El Niño preparedness, improving early warning systems, and taking swift measures to mitigate the impact of disasters.\n\nThe meeting comes as Somalia faces increasing climate-related shocks, including droughts and floods, which have been linked to El Niño phenomena in the past. SoDMA has been working with international partners to bolster the country's disaster preparedness and response capabilities. FAO has been a key partner in providing technical support and resources for early warning and food security monitoring.\n\nNorway's involvement reflects broader international interest in supporting Somalia's resilience efforts. The Norwegian government has previously funded humanitarian and climate adaptation projects in the Horn of Africa. The meeting underscored the importance of coordinated action among national authorities, UN agencies, and donor countries to anticipate and respond to climate risks.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is significant as many have relatives in regions prone to flooding and drought. Diaspora communities often mobilize resources during emergencies, and improved early warning systems can help them better coordinate their support. Additionally, diaspora investment in affected areas can benefit from enhanced disaster risk reduction measures.\n\nThe talks also highlighted the need for strengthened early warning systems, which are critical for timely evacuation and resource allocation. SoDMA and its partners are expected to continue collaboration on contingency planning and community awareness. The outcomes of the meeting may lead to joint initiatives to address El Niño-related risks in the coming months.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/19/sodma-fao-iyo-dowladda-norway-oo-ka-wada-hadlay-xoojinta-u-diyaargarowga-musiibooyinka-la-xiriira-el-nino/",
