    "summary": "A report by the Wesleyan Media Project reveals that over $137 million was spent on political advertisements using artificial intelligence during the 2026 U.S. midterm elections, with 220 such ads identified across 34 states.",
    "content": "More than $137 million was spent on political advertisements that utilized artificial intelligence (AI) during the 2026 U.S. midterm election campaign, according to a report released by the Wesleyan Media Project. The study identified 220 political ads that employed AI, broadcast across 34 states. These ads included videos, images, and voices that were either generated or altered using AI technology.\n\nThe report highlights the growing use of AI in political campaigns, reflecting how technological tools are increasingly shaping electioneering and political competition. The Wesleyan Media Project, a research organization that tracks political advertising, documented the extent of AI involvement in the midterms, providing one of the first comprehensive assessments of its kind.\n\nAI-generated content in political ads can include deepfakes—manipulated videos or audio that mimic real people—as well as synthetic voices and imagery. While such tools can be used for creative purposes, they also raise concerns about misinformation and the potential to deceive voters. The study's findings underscore the need for transparency and regulation as AI becomes more accessible to campaigns.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, particularly those with ties to the United States, the findings are significant. Many diaspora members follow U.S. politics closely due to family, business, or civic connections. The rise of AI in political advertising could affect how they receive and interpret information about candidates and issues, especially if they rely on social media or international news sources. Misleading AI-generated content could distort perceptions and undermine trust in democratic processes.\n\nAdditionally, the diaspora's own engagement in political discourse—both in the U.S. and in Somalia—may be influenced by the spread of AI tools. As Somalis around the world navigate an increasingly digital information landscape, understanding the role of AI in political messaging becomes crucial for informed participation.\n\nThe Wesleyan Media Project's report serves as a timely reminder of the evolving nature of political communication and the challenges it presents for voters everywhere.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/19/mareykanka-137-milyan-oo-lagu-bixiyey-xayeysiisyo-siyaasadeed-oo-ai-loo-adeegsaday/",
